[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was merged as one issue with one fix queued. Read report by report, it
holds three problems. The queued parser fix closes only the first one.

<Callout kind="warn">
Not one issue. Each problem below has its own ticket and its own fix. Do not close the other two
when the parser fix ships.
</Callout>

## The split

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {issues} />

Counts are unique reports. The export lists some reports twice (overlapping pages), so the table
counts by id, the same way the kb's other tables do.

## 1 · `parser`: the shared parser reads dates month first

Every tenant on #4471 writes the day first. The shared parser reads every date month first, so
`07/04` (7 April) becomes 4 July. The bodies show a clean swap: the day and the month trade places.
These come in by app, email and the booking-v2 form, from every tenant.

**Fix:** parse with the tenant's locale, not a fixed month-first order.

## 2 · `hint`: booking-v3 tells people to type month first

booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People follow the hint and type the month first,
and the form then reads the date the tenant's way, day first. The swap looks the same as `parser`,
but the parser is not involved. Only reports sent **through the form** of a **booking-v3** tenant
are this problem; the same tenants' app and email reports are `parser`.

**Fix:** print the hint from the tenant's locale (`DD/MM/YYYY` for every tenant here). The parser fix
does not change this.

## 3 · `one-day`: dates move by one day, not by a swap

These reports move the date by one day or depend on where the reader is: "Tuesday 12 May" in the
invite, Monday 11 May in the calendar; "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto got Monday";
"every booking in June is one day off". No month/day swap explains a one-day move. The desk had
already read them this way ([desk notes](/kb/incidents/4471): "read as a one-day move") but filed
them under `parser` by tenant.

**Fix:** look at how the invite turns a booking time into a calendar date across time zones
(midnight and all-day bookings, daylight saving). The parser fix does not change this.

## Why it stayed one ticket

- [Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject. Subjects here say "wrong
  day" for all three problems, so the subject cannot split them.
- Intake's `hint` tag guesses from the browser. It disagrees with the tenant and form often, in both
  directions. Sort by tenant, form and body, not by the tag.

